-----

design discussion:

    this is a jitter buffer for RTP packets based on C++11 and STL.

    Memory management is something to be wary of; we will store our own
    pointer to given packets in our buffer and will return copies of these
    pointers when packets are popped off the buffer.  In the case of a
    buffer reset, or similar activity, we will go ahead and delete any
    packets in our buffer at the time.

    This implementation assumes that it is operating in a multi-threaded
    environment and syncronizes all operations/methods.

    Requirements:
        R0: eliminate effects of jitter in RTP packet arrival
        R1: detect and track missing packets in RTP stream
        R2: allow configuration of depth and packet size (in milliseconds)
        R3: track and report ongoing jitter stats:
                dropped packets
                out of order packets
                missed packets
                jitter (lifetime)
                max jitter (lifetime)
                current depth

    The RTPJitter will manage an internal buffer of RTP frames.  The application
    layer adds and removes packets from this buffer through the .push() and
    .pop() interface.  It is up to the application to manage and schedule this
    process on its own thread.

    A jitter buffer introduces a configured delay in the delivery and
    processing of packets; this is the "depth" in milliseconds of the buffer.
    Once the first packet is received into an empty buffer, the "depth" timer
    starts.  Once it expires, packets will be delivered upon request in the
    .pop() function until the buffer becomes empty again.

    The remaining logic involves how to handle newly arriving packets and
    handle the exception cases: missing or out-of-order packets.  ... and how
    to detect and address jitter.

    Jitter will be calculated as the mean deviation from the expected based on
    details, definitions, and sample code from RFC3550 section 6.4.1 and
    Appendix A.8.

    The jitter buffer will not examine, or differentiate, packets based on SSRC.


    note 1 - we use a std::deque to implement the internal buffer because it
        allows us to ignore the details of memory management for the buffer
        as it may grow and shrink.




-----

fixed-rate RTP repair (RFC 4588 style):

    RTPJitter can additionally run in a "repair" mode for a fixed-rate
    media stream that has a dedicated RTX SSRC/payload type.  The mode is
    self contained: packet arrival, missing-packet feedback and playout all
    live on the same RTPJitter object and address slots by original (media)
    sequence number.  No external queue or secondary jitter buffer is needed.

    Configuration:

        RepairConfig cfg;
        cfg.primary_ssrc = ...;   // media stream SSRC
        cfg.rtx_ssrc     = ...;   // retransmission SSRC (must differ)
        cfg.primary_pt   = ...;   // media payload type
        cfg.rtx_pt       = ...;   // retransmission payload type (must differ)
        cfg.clock_rate   = 8000;  // samples per second
        cfg.packet_ms    = 20;    // fixed packet duration
        cfg.delay_ms     = 60;    // playout delay after the anchor packet
        cfg.retry_ms     = 40;    // per-hole NACK repeat interval
        cfg.window_packets = 8;   // 2..512, slots reachable from the playout head

        if (!jitter.configure_repair(cfg)) { ... }   // false == no state change

    configure_repair() requires distinct SSRCs and payload types (PT in
    0..127), positive clock_rate and retry_ms, packet_ms in 1..65535, non
    negative delay_ms, window_packets in 2..512 and a whole-number
    clock_rate * packet_ms / 1000.  A rejected configuration leaves the object
    exactly as it was; an accepted one discards any buffered legacy stream and
    timing state, then activates repair mode.  reset() clears the stream and
    timing state but keeps the repair configuration.

    Call order:

        jitter.configure_repair(cfg);
        //   ... time now_us advances, non negative and monotonic ...
        jitter.push_at(packet, now_us);          // media and RTX arrivals
        jitter.nacks_at(now_us);                 // poll holes to request
        jitter.pop_at(&out, now_us);             // player pulls one slot

    The first accepted media packet defines the anchor: arrival A, sequence
    S and timestamp T.  Slot k then has original sequence S+k (16 bit wrap),
    media timestamp T + k*clock_rate*packet_ms/1000 (32 bit wrap) and playout
    time A + delay_ms*1000 + k*packet_ms*1000.  RTX packets arriving before
    the anchor are ignored.

    push_at() accepts an original packet or an RTX packet.  A gap is revealed
    when an accepted packet advances the highest known sequence, or when a
    restored RTX packet fills a hole; nothing beyond the highest known
    sequence is ever requested.  Only slots from the playout head through
    window_packets - 1 are accepted: older slots and mismatched timestamps
    are BAD_PACKET, slots beyond the window are BUFFER_OVERFLOW, and neither
    changes state.  Out-of-order arrivals are fine as long as the active
    sequence span stays below 32768.  The first accepted content for a slot
    wins; later duplicates (media or RTX) return SUCCESS without changing
    content, depth or timing.  A repair may arrive up to, and including, the
    slot's playout instant; one microsecond later it is BAD_PACKET even if
    pop_at() has not been called yet.

    pop_at() handles one slot at a time.  Before the deadline it returns
    BUFFERING; at or after the deadline it returns SUCCESS and advances when
    the slot holds a packet, or DROPPED_PACKET and advances when it does not.
    Past the highest known slot it returns BUFFER_EMPTY without advancing.
    The output argument is only written on SUCCESS.  get_depth()/get_depth_ms()
    report retained packets and packets times packet_ms.

    RTX packets carry the original sequence number as a two byte big endian
    OSN at the start of the payload (RFC 4588).  The outer SSRC and payload
    type must match the RTX configuration; the outer sequence number is
    ignored.  CSRCs, header extension and padding are skipped correctly, and
    malformed envelopes (bad version, truncated header/extension, invalid
    padding, payload shorter than the OSN) are BAD_PACKET without side
    effects.  Before delivery the OSN is removed and sequence/SSRC/payload
    type are restored to media values; marker, timestamp, CSRCs, extension,
    media bytes and valid padding are preserved and nLen/payload_bytes match
    the restored packet.  The caller's input object is never modified.

    nacks_at() returns original sequence numbers of revealed, unfilled,
    unexpired holes in playout order.  A hole is reported on its first query,
    then at most once per retry_ms; repeated queries in the same instant do
    not re-report, and a late query reports once without replaying missed
    rounds.  A packet arriving for a hole cancels its retries immediately;
    consumed or expired holes never reappear.  Querying never advances playout
    or rebuilds the anchor.

    While repair mode is active the legacy push()/pop() return BAD_PACKET and
    leave state untouched.  Without a configuration, push_at()/pop_at() return
    BAD_PACKET and nacks_at() returns an empty vector.  All repair timing is
    supplied by the caller in microseconds; the implementation uses no wall
    clock, threads or sleeps.
